 2. The Output: EU KIDS Act, AI labels, U.K. push 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Brendan Lynch/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Here's our guide to catch you up on the AI policy news you may have missed this week:

 🤖 AI services portal: Trump administration officials are planning an AI-powered website that could let Americans renew passports and file taxes through a chatbot, the Washington Sun reports .

 Former DOGE officials are part of the project, which could launch as early as the end of this month. 🇪🇺 Kids' online safety: The European Commission this week proposed the EU KIDS Act , sweeping legislation that would limit minors' access to social media platforms and AI chatbots.

 AI companions would be barred from carrying a child's earlier conversation into later ones and from simulating human relationships "in ways likely to create emotional dependency." Providers would need to demonstrate compliance before making AI companions and chatbots available to EU users or face fines of up to 6% of their global annual revenue. 🇬🇧 AI bill push: A U.K. parliamentary committee called for a wide-ranging new AI law, citing human rights risks.

 The report recommended mandatory transparency requirements and a new regulatory body with enforcement powers. "The most dangerous uses of AI should be banned, and others should be regulated in a principled and risk-based way," the report stated. 🐻 California bill watch: Gov. Gavin Newsom (D) signed a law requiring ads that use AI-generated performers to say so.

 The measure applies to video and audio ads and bars their continued use if they don't include the required disclosure. 
 
